  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">High quality Egyptian onions for (new) export markets: a supply chain approach</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Egypt has favourable production circumstances for onions. The vast majority of onions is produced for the local market. Due to early harvesting and relatively proximity, the EU and Eastern Europe offer interesting markets for 5 months per year. During the remaining months, export to the Middle East forms an important outlet. The potential to benefit from these markets is not maximised due to sub-optimal management throughout the chain.The objective of this project is to strengthen the entire value chain, from primary production (outgrowers) to post harvest handling, grading and retail packing in a modern HACCP-certified pack house, and forwarding to European retailers and the Middle East. This will go hand in hand with training and introduction of quality control standards across the chain, including GlobalGAP for farmers who are organised in the Development Association of Small Organic Growers (DASOG). The pack house will be in Fayoum or Sadat City. At the end of the pilot annual production will be 12,000 tonnes.Applicant is Gourmet B.V. (1988) with local partner Pharaonic Bioherb Co. Ltd (2002) will set up a joint venture and will hold each 50% of the shares.Third partner is ENZA Zaden Export B.V.. ENZA Zaden Export B.V. will be involved in introduction of new seeds and trials. An important role in the supply chain is also reserved for DASOG, which unites 800 growers in the Fayoum and the Delta.The project is innovative, as it will establish a modern and HACCP-certified grading and packing facility for onion exports. It will also introduce new onion varieties and cultivation methods at the level of primary producers. This project is expected to boost the onion export sector at large, as it will serve as an example of a modern integrated supply chain approach.</narrative>
  </description>
